        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_51675c9eb5cf40e4a154980d813205b3",
      "statement": "On verification failure the overseer offers exactly one bounded re-prompt (MAX_CONFIG_REPROMPTS=1) so the user can repair in place, then denies with one line per failed binding naming the connection and account; on any throw it rolls back observers registered during this call (keeping pre-existing registrations, since removing them would break excludeObservers while the persisted record still asserts them), and persists the observer record only after all addObserver calls succeed.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://packages/workshop-backend/src/overseer.ts#L7996-L8035",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:01b7cf53b8c2d7227c3b6d12a0a95b6883f8277e6827c02242b0c970b414d6d2:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_f62fec2d862d4faaa8770b8d550fd90e",
      "statement": "The verifier pattern: the observer's own chosen account mints an opaque GatekeeperUserVerifier which the overseer passes only back to the same vendor's gatekeeper; because the runtime cannot unwrap a Fetcher to its props, the gatekeeper implements a public but non-standard method on its verifier that its own addObserver may call, and the server-side vendor match in getVerifier enforces the pairing.",
      "evidence": [
